     1	# **Mobile Bug Report Reproduction via Global Search on the** **App UI Model**
     2	
     3	[ZHAOXU ZHANG,](HTTPS://ORCID.ORG/0000-0003-1333-1637) University of Southern California, USA
     4	[FAZLE MOHAMMED TAWSIF,](HTTPS://ORCID.ORG/0000-0002-4757-4170) University of Southern California, USA
     5	[KOMEI RYU,](HTTPS://ORCID.ORG/0009-0005-0199-3424) University of Southern California, USA
     6	[TINGTING YU,](HTTPS://ORCID.ORG/0000-0002-9461-4251) University of Connecticut, USA
     7	[WILLIAM G. J. HALFOND,](HTTPS://ORCID.ORG/0000-0003-4951-9367) University of Southern California, USA
     8	
     9	
    10	Bug report reproduction is an important, but time-consuming task carried out during mobile app maintenance.
    11	To accelerate this task, current research has proposed automated reproduction techniques that rely on a guided
    12	dynamic exploration of the app to match bug report steps with UI events in a mobile app. However, these
    13	techniques struggle to find the correct match when the bug reports have missing or inaccurately described
    14	steps. To address these limitations, we propose a new bug report reproduction technique that uses an app’s UI
    15	model to perform a global search across all possible matches between steps and UI actions and identify the
    16	most likely match while accounting for the possibility of missing or inaccurate steps. To do this, our approach
    17	redefines the bug report reproduction process as a Markov model and finds the best paths through the model
    18	using a dynamic programming based technique. We conducted an empirical evaluation on 72 real-world bug
    19	reports. Our approach achieved a 94% reproduction rate on the total bug reports and a 93% reproduction rate
    20	on bug reports with missing steps, significantly outperforming the state-of-the-art approaches. Our approach
    21	was also more effective in finding the matches from the steps to UI events than the state-of-the-art approaches.
    22	
