    65	
    66	117:2 Zhaoxu Zhang, Fazle Mohammed Tawsif, Komei Ryu, Tingting Yu, and William G. J. Halfond
    67	
    68	
    69	the bug report manually. Even in cases where the provided steps are well-written, the process
    70	can still be time-consuming since modern apps generally have complex user interfaces ( UI s) and
    71	support many similar sequences of UI events on different screens, each of which may or may not
    72	lead to the reported failure. As a result, developers may need to exhaustively explore the app to
    73	identify the correct events for reproduction. These challenges can make bug report reproduction
    74	a time-consuming and error-prone task for developers, potentially causing a significant delay in
    75	debugging the app.
    76	To accelerate the reproduction process, the software engineering community has proposed
    77	automated techniques for reproducing bug reports of mobile applications. Given the reproduction
    78	steps in a bug report, these techniques dynamically explore the app and attempt to sequentially
    79	match each step against a UI event in the app. However, the low quality of bug reports can cause
    80	problems for automated reproduction. If a step is described inaccurately or vaguely, an automated
    81	approach may incorrectly match it with the wrong UI event. This can cause unintended UIs to
    82	appear and make it impossible to match subsequent steps. If the bug report has missing steps, a
    83	similar problem may occur. In this situation, the approach prematurely tries to match a step against
    84	the incorrect UI event, unaware that additional events are required to properly identify the event
    85	that matches the step accurately. These situations can lead to automated approaches needing more
    86	time to search for the correct reproducing events or cause them to fail completely if they are unable
    87	to account for the shortcomings of the bug reports.
    88	Existing state-of-the-art approaches for bug report reproduction, ReCDroid [ 52 ], Yakusu [ 24 ]
    89	and ReproBot [ 50 ], attempt to address these challenges. However, these approaches have limitations
    90	that limit their overall effectiveness. To find a sequence of UI events that match with the given
    91	steps, ReCDroid and Yakusu employ a greedy search based strategy to navigate through an app’s
    92	UIs. Inaccurate or missing steps can cause this strategy to incorrectly prioritize the exploration
    93	of paths with initially high similarity between steps and UI events, but that ultimately do not
    94	lead to an overall correct match. These approaches also incorporate heuristics in the search when
    95	they are unable to find a correct matching path, but in the worst case, their search may reduce
    96	to an exhaustive exploration of the app, which is very time-consuming. ReproBot improves this
    97	search process by utilizing a reinforcement learning ( RL ) based algorithm to guide the search. This
    98	allows the search to consider sequences of events that exhibit less similarity at the beginning but
    99	eventually lead to overall better matches. Nonetheless, the RL based approach cannot guarantee
   100	that it will find the best matching sequence since it is, by definition, non-deterministic. As we
   101	show in Section 4, the sub-optimal search strategies employed by these techniques can reduce their
   102	overall effectiveness in reproducing bug reports.
   103	To address these limitations, we introduce a new approach for finding the UI events that best
   104	match the steps of a bug report. At a high level, our approach utilizes a model of the app’s UI s to
   105	search for the global best-matched UI events to reproduce the steps. Since the search space in the UI
   106	model for this best match is large, our approach defines a Markov model to describe the possible ways
   107	to match steps with UI events and then uses a dynamic-programming based technique to efficiently
   108	find the best matches. In addition, our approach further uses the UI model to effectively bridge the
   109	possible missing steps in the bug report and discover the complete event paths for reproduction. We
   110	implemented our approach in a prototype tool, Roam, and compared its performance against the
   111	three aforementioned state-of-the-art approaches for bug report reproduction. The results of this
   112	evaluation showed that our approach achieved 94% reproduction rate on the subject bug reports,
   113	outperforming all three existing techniques. Our approach was particularly effective, as compared
   114	to the other approaches, when trying to reproduce bug reports with missing steps. Overall, these
   115	results were very positive and indicated that our approach to finding the best UI event matches for
   116	a bug report can lead to overall better bug report reproduction.
   117	
